  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 319 AM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nFor today, there will be a decrease in monsoon moisture across the \narea.  However, lingering moisture will still allow for sct aftn and \nearly evening showers/storms across the higher terrain mainly along \nand south of I-70 and across South Park.  Although PW's values will \nbe lower today, a few storms may still produce locally heavier \nrainfall.  Across the plains expect dry conditions with warmer \ntemperatures as readings rise into the mid to upper 90's.\n\nFor tonight, most tstm activity should end by mid evening over the \nhigher terrain.  Meanwhile outflow boundaries from higher terrain \nactivity could trigger a few evening showers/storms across wrn \nportions of the I-25 Corridor from Denver south across the Palmer \nDivide. \n\nFor Sat and Sun, an upper level high will be over the area.  As a \nresult, will see very hot temperatures across nern CO both days, \nwith Heat Advisory criteria, for the I-25 Corridor. Highs will be\nin the upper 90's to lower 100's. Record highs could be broken in\nsome locations both days. As far as tstm chances, latest data \ncontinues to show a gradual increase in monsoon moisture from Sat \ninto Sun with slowly rising PW values. Thus will see a gradual \nincrease in tstm activity over the higher terrain with slow moving\nstorms and locally heavy ranfall possible especially by Sun. \nAcross the plains, outflow boundaries from tstms over the higher \nterrain may trigger some widely sct tstms both Sat and Sun during \nthe late aftn/early evening hours along the I-25 Corridor. \n\nLooking ahead to Mon thru Wed of next week, the upper level high may \nshift slightly southward.  As a result, this will allow for monsoon \nmoisture to move across the area with weak disturbances likely \nembedded in the mid level flow. PW's will be 150% to 175% percent\nabove normal. Overall, there will be a good chance of showers and\nstorms each aftn and evening across the area, based on the latest\ndata. Meanwhile, temps won't be quite as hot during the period \nbut readings will still remain mostly in the 90's over nern CO.",
